**Graph:** a sine curve. The force is zero at 0°, rises to a maximum at 90°, falls to zero at 180°, reaches a maximum magnitude in the opposite direction at 270°, and returns to zero at 360°. (Plotting the magnitude only, giving two positive humps with peaks at 90° and 270°, is also acceptable.)

**Justification:** the force on the wire is $F = lIB\sin\theta$, where $\theta$ is the angle between the wire and the field. $l$, $I$ and $B$ are constant, so $F \propto \sin\theta$:
- at $\theta = 0^\circ, 180^\circ, 360^\circ$ the wire is parallel to the field, $\sin\theta = 0$, so $F = 0$;
- at $\theta = 90^\circ, 270^\circ$ the wire is perpendicular to the field, $\sin\theta = \pm1$, so $F$ has its maximum magnitude $lIB$;
- between 180° and 360° the current direction relative to the field is reversed, so the force (into or out of the page, by the right-hand rule) acts in the opposite direction.
